{"name":"Wahoo","hex":"#272d4e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wahoo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A ghost town in California.","An unincorporated community in Florida."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wahoo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wahoo","blocks":[{"text":"The wahoo is a scombrid fish found worldwide in tropical and subtropical seas. In Hawaii, the wahoo is known as ono. The species is sometimes called hoo in the United States. It is best known to sports fishermen, as its speed and high quality makes it a prized and valued game fish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Acanthocybium_solandri.png/330px-Acanthocybium_solandri.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wahoo"},{"title":"Wahoo (board game)","blocks":[{"text":"Wahoo is a cross and circle board game similar to Parchisi that involves moving a set number of marbles around the board, trying to get them into the safety zone. The game is alleged to have originated in the Appalachian hills, but it is nearly identical to Mensch ärgere Dich nicht, a German board game originating in 1907. Most boards are used by two to four players. Wahoo has been a popular game for decades. Even today, custom-made boards proliferate on eBay and game manufacturer Parker Brothers has sold their own version of the game, under the title Aggravation, for decades."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Aggravation_board.jpg/330px-Aggravation_board.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wahoo_(board_game)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg/1280px-Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcanthocybium_solandri_733521835.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg/1280px-Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Head closeup","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcanthocybium_solandri_42717896.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg/1280px-Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg/500px-Acanthocybium_solandri_%28Cuvier%2C_1832%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At Tenerife, Canary Islands","link":"https://en.